@ id: g402
@ order: 402
@ title: Thinking mode
@ subtitle: emit a reasoning trace before the answer — the reasoning lever
@ source: arxiv 2607.02770

--- note
## The mechanism
Gemma 4 adds a **thinking mode**: before producing the final response, the model first generates a
**reasoning trace** (an internal chain of thought), and only then answers. This is the same idea
popularised by reasoning models (the report cites OpenAI, 2024). The trace is a first-class part of
generation, carried in a dedicated **thought channel** in the chat format (see g409): the model writes
`<|channel>thought ...<channel|>` and then the user-visible answer.

Why it helps: reasoning-heavy tasks (mathematics, competitive coding, science) benefit from the model
"spending tokens to think" — it can decompose, plan, and check work before committing to an answer,
instead of answering in one forward pass.

--- note
## Turning it on
Thinking is a **toggle**, activated by placing a `<|think|>` control token in a leading **system
turn**. All Gemma 4 models share one tokenizer; a handful of control tokens are reserved for this
instruction-tuned (IT) formatting. So the *same weights* run with or without a visible reasoning
trace, depending on the prompt. (Full formatting example in **g409 / Table 11**.)

--- note
## The evidence
The report says most of its benchmark numbers are reported **in thinking mode** (Table 5 header:
"All models are in thinking mode unless explicitly stated"). The reasoning-heavy jumps over the prior
generation are large — e.g. **AIME 2026** goes from 20.8 (Gemma 3 27B) to **89.2** (Gemma 4 31B), and
**Codeforces Elo** from 110 to **2150**. Those gains are the combined effect of a better base model
*and* thinking mode; the report does not isolate thinking-mode's marginal contribution with an
ablation, so we take the table at face value: thinking-mode models are strong on STEM/coding.

